 <table width="100%" border="0" bgcolor="#FFFFFF" cellpadding="5">
  <tr>
    <td background="images/bg.gif"><font class=""><a href="modules.php?name=News&new_topic=35">Life</a></font><br><font class="testo_notizia" color="#000000"><b>Reflections on a lifelong love/hate relationship with the Material Girl.</b></font></td>
  </tr>
  <tr height="1">
    <td  background="images/riga.gif"></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td bgcolor="#FFFFFF">
	<div align=justify><font class="storytext"><P><STRONG>03.29.05 By Kimberly Nichols / GW</STRONG></P>
<P>In New York a few years back, my daughter and I were sitting on the balcony of a friend named Lance who lives overlooking Central Park. <STRONG>We watched the street as a familiar woman and child came walking into the apartment building</STRONG>. It was <STRONG>Madonna</STRONG>. Lance pointed her out and my daughter asked <STRONG>“Who is that?”</STRONG> as if she truly didn’t know. Not only did this make me feel old, but <STRONG>I realized how much Madonna is an icon from my lifetime, whether I wanted to admit it or not.</STRONG> Now I know my gay friends may resent me for this admission, but I am one of those people who had had enough of Madonna for one lifetime. <STRONG>She popped onto the music scene</STRONG> when we were immersed in influential puberty and stuck around to taunt us with her growth and foibles, exploits and behaviors, in parallel with our own lives for years. <STRONG>A lot of girls like me grew up in a love hate relationship with Madonna because she represented the things that were both worst and best in us as we matured.</STRONG> Sometimes, <STRONG>a role model</STRONG>, sometimes <STRONG>an embarrassing cousin, she never failed to scoop</STRONG> us along with her in the limelight as our mirror. It seems like just yesterday that I was begging my mom’s cool hairdresser friends to perm my hair into oblivion after bleaching it full of misplaced streaks, <STRONG>so that I too, could look like a virgin</STRONG>. Her<STRONG> crucifixes</STRONG>, silver bangles and black jelly bracelets slammed together onto her wrists like tambourines<STRONG> were the stuff of dreamy rebellion for us back then</STRONG>. The lace and the make-up, the black cut off leggings, it was sexier than punk rock and sooty like reading playboys in your father’s basement. She was revolutionary and unconventional and we applauded the self-esteem in her vulgarity and outrageous attitude. <STRONG>When she married the original rebel Sean Penn, we knew she was our heroine</STRONG>. But then things changed and she lost us. It was around the time of her wedding dress and bustier phase, the time of her bedtime stories where she would sit on a tufted bed ala Queen Anne and admonish audience members for talking. <STRONG>Like a royal wench, she mounted her pop throne and forgot the little people and that meant all of us original fans.</STRONG> But throughout the years we still watched her like a little sister gone astray in the cheerleader crowd. We saw her experiments in identity take her through the <STRONG>Papa Don’t Preach</STRONG> and Jesus Fuck Me years. By the time she produced <STRONG>her shock-block book SEX we were over her</STRONG>. We considered her a shrewd <STRONG>business woman no doubt but no longer had interest in her artistic integrity</STRONG> and besides, she had the gay boys who would forever bump and grind to her moods. We would admit now and then, that she could still make us dance but that was a casual relationship in disco clubs at two a.m. “Music makes the people come together… yeah.” She remained a twinkle in our memories as Gwen Stefani stepped in to steal the crown of spiked-Cinderella-pop-culture-girl. When she started her Integrity Tour in the 1990’s we heard her spout yoga and motherhood as she turned into a silent, more peaceful-seeming version of her former self. We decided to like her again for a minute. <STRONG>And then she found Kabbalah</STRONG>, the latest in our revolving fascination with religions, and as much as we wanted to shut the door on her again for being so gullible, we didn’t this time<STRONG>. As I saw the myriad of pictures of her wearing the red string of Kabbalah, I realized that just like us she is forever searching.</STRONG> The other day my daughter ran into the kitchen as I was talking to a friend and asked me who that old lady was who lived next to Lance in New York. I smiled, and then <STRONG>flipping through the pages of a magazine in front of me, came face to face with Madonna at fifty, gracing a Versace ad, looking peaceful and wide eyed and sophisticated and mature. </STRONG></P>
</font></div>
	</td>
  </tr>
