 <table width="100%" border="0" bgcolor="#FFFFFF" cellpadding="5">
  <tr>
    <td background="images/bg.gif"><font class=""><a href="modules.php?name=News&new_topic=39">Kabbalah</a></font><br><font class="testo_notizia" color="#000000"><b>Exploring Kabbalah: Chernobyl, the Water and the Bracelets.</b></font></td>
  </tr>
  <tr height="1">
    <td  background="images/riga.gif"></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td bgcolor="#FFFFFF">
	<div align=justify><font class="storytext"><P><IMG src="/kabbalah.jpg" align=right border=0>Before publishing the first essey on <STRONG>Kabbalah</STRONG> by <STRONG>J. Stephen</STRONG>, we asked him to&nbsp;explain <STRONG>briefly</STRONG> some more points.</P>
<P><STRONG><IMG src="/images/rombo.jpg" border=0>&nbsp;The Chernobyl thing</STRONG>. That is a tradition during <STRONG>'connections'</STRONG>, or <STRONG>group meditations</STRONG>, during which <STRONG>the men shout out the names of places where people are suffering, where Light is lacking, where creation has gone astray</STRONG>. <STRONG>Chernobyl</STRONG> is certainly one of them. But you will also hear cries of <STRONG>Baghdad</STRONG>! <STRONG>Jerusalem</STRONG>! <STRONG>Sierra Leone</STRONG>! 
<P><STRONG><IMG src="/images/rombo.jpg" border=0>&nbsp;The water thing</STRONG>. <STRONG>There is a lot of science in Kabbalah, lots of complex metaphysics</STRONG>. There is an idea that, through applied consciousness, <STRONG>the actual physical properties of matter can be enhanced or even changed</STRONG>. There is scientific support for this, even if it seems mostly theoretical. <STRONG>By meditating on certain ancient combinations of Hebrew letters, Kabbalah water is enhanced with rejuvenating properties</STRONG>. Now this may seem hard to believe, and yet we buy things all the time because advertisers tell us that they will make us feel good or give us energy. Why do people believe that a soft drink full of chemicals and additives would be good for them, and doubt that spring water could be blessed by ancient technology? The same prople who believe that Moses really did part the Red Sea (even though we know he was no where near the Red Sea but was actually at a 'sea of reeds') doubt that he used the <STRONG>72 Names Of God</STRONG> to do so. I don't know everything. But Kabbalah has opened my mind to extraordinary possibilities. And then, <STRONG>if you want proof, just consider the lives of people who do believe</STRONG>. Something about drinking all that Kabbalah water seems to agree with Madge. She's never been so healthy. <STRONG>Kabbalah is not about magical hocus-pocus. It's very practical really</STRONG>. There are some strange sounding rituals, there is <STRONG>astrology</STRONG> and <STRONG>numerology</STRONG>. <STRONG>But there are also some ancient concepts that have been proven by science</STRONG>. There was a time when no one Kabbalists believe in the atom. <STRONG>Einstein</STRONG>'s theory of relativity was inspired by <STRONG>Zoharic theory</STRONG>. Kabbalah has been applying the concept of <STRONG>string theory</STRONG> to the universe for thousands of years. <STRONG>In Kabbalah, science and spirit are the same thing</STRONG>. Slowly, mush of the scientific and medical communities are beginning to appreciate that idea. And even if you don't believe in it, it is fascinating stuff. 
<P><STRONG><IMG src="/images/rombo.jpg" border=0>&nbsp;The red bracelet</STRONG>.<STRONG> Kabbalah is really not a cult</STRONG>. Most Kabbalah students are very careful not to 'convert' or 'recruit' people, even as we strive to 'share the flame.' <STRONG>There is no membership in Kabbalah</STRONG>. The so-called <STRONG>"Kabbalah bracelets"</STRONG> the <STRONG>Red Strings</STRONG> we wear, do not indicate inclusion in some kind of club. </P><img src="/corrispondenti/co-j_stephen.jpg" border="0">
</font></div>
	</td>
  </tr>
